- Story & Context: Koji is an initiate who keeps his dreams in a rusted filing cabinet, mourning a lost date on a paper calendar that only lists days that never occurred. He touches the cold metal of the drawer and finds it vibrates in sync with the subterranean Hammond melody of the monastery's daily vespers. "I marked the Tuesday that was taken away" is his only defense when the Abbot asks why his patches are so dangerously erratic.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary man standing in a vast, dark library of rusted metal cabinets, paper calendars peeling off the walls like dead skin, grainy texture, cyan and deep ochre tones.

Track 3: What Do The Solder-Stains Say?
- Story & Context: Legend says the monastery will vanish when the last dream is transcribed, turning all the monks into sentient circuitry wired into the floorboards. Travelers visiting the ruins claim they hear a low-frequency hum, an after-myth of the music once played, asking a simple question to the incoming tide. "Does the machine still bleed if the hands stop moving?" the wall seems to vibrate, answering the pilgrims who dare to listen to the silent hum.
